;;; Lazyness.mon --- A gentle tour of call-by-need evaluation

:author   Laluxx
:version  1.0.0
:keywords lazy call-by-need delay force sharing evaluation

;;; Commentary:

 Laziness is a choice about =when= a pure computation runs. Monad is eager by
 default: arguments are ordinarily evaluated before a function receives them.
 The expression

                 delay (expensive-answer 40 2)

 is different. =delay= packages the expression as a value of type =Lazy Int=
 without running it. Nothing asks for the integer until =force= demands it.

 This is call-by-need, not merely call-by-name. The first successful =force=
 evaluates the suspended expression and remembers its result. Every later
 force of the same Lazy value observes that shared result. The computation is
 delayed once and evaluated at most once.

         expression ──delay──> Lazy value ──force──> result
                                       ╰──force──> same result

 The sharing guarantee is important even in pure code: it prevents repeated
 work. Effects are intentionally excluded from =delay= because memoizing an IO
 action would silently change how many times that effect occurs.

 A Lazy wrapper is shallow. Forcing =Lazy (Lazy Int)= removes one layer only;
 the inner computation remains suspended until it too is forced. This makes
 laziness compositional: a data structure chooses exactly which recursive
 positions are delayed instead of turning the whole language lazy.

 =map= and =liftA2= preserve suspension. They describe work beneath a Lazy
 wrapper; they do not perform it. =TotalDemand= is the companion proof surface:
 it records that a particular demand is known to terminate, allowing
 =demand-total= to expose the value without a latent divergence effect.

;;; Code:

import Control.Lazy
import Control.Evaluation

module LazynessExample where


define expensive-answer :: Int -> Int -> Int
  left right -> left + right


define delayed-answer :: Lazy Int
  delay (expensive-answer 40 2)


define demand-twice :: Lazy Int -> Int
  value -> (force value) + (force value)


define delayed-double :: Lazy Int
  delay ((force delayed-answer) * 2)


define nested-delay :: Lazy (Lazy Int)
  delay (delay 42)


define safely-chosen :: Bool -> Lazy a -> Lazy a -> a
  True wanted _ -> force wanted
  False _ wanted -> force wanted


tests
  assert-eq (force delayed-answer)                          42 "force demands a delayed computation"
  assert-eq (demand-twice delayed-answer)                   84 "repeated force observes one shared result"
  assert-eq (force delayed-double)                          84 "derived work can remain beneath Lazy"
  assert-eq (force (force nested-delay))                    42 "force removes one Lazy layer at a time"
  assert-eq (safely-chosen True (delay 42) (delay (1 / 0))) 42 "an unchosen suspension is not evaluated"
